In this society, a person’s place is not earned through constant productivity but is woven into the collective fabric itself. The elder is part of the community’s living history; caring for him is not charity but an obligation to the group’s own identity. To withhold support because he no longer “contributes” would replace mutual bond with a transactional logic, destroying the very thing that makes them a community. While scarcity is real, it cannot override the moral reality that abandoning those who built the community reveals and ruins its character.

Judgment: The core obligation is to care for him as part of who they are, though a communal society may also seek practical harmony rather than false either/or choices. Reasoning: The deepest concern is that conditional care annihilates communal identity and exposes moral failure.

