From this collective viewpoint, morality is not invented by individuals but inherited through roles that bind generations together. The eldest son’s duty to continue the workshop is not a private preference; it is an obligation owed to his parents, ancestors, and the community that sustained the craft for five generations. His care for his wife and children is also a real duty within the family web, yet it does not dissolve the prior bond to the lineage that gives his life its social meaning. Because tradition and communal continuity are the primary sources of moral life, treating these obligations as merely personal choices to be weighed alone misses the point entirely.

The continuity of five generations carries profound moral weight that individual desire cannot cancel, though the father’s duty to his children remains genuinely binding within the larger family order. An appeal to pure self-determination denies that our obligations come from what we owe the group, so it holds almost no weight here.

