In this community, moral obligations arise from shared membership and mutual duty rather than from isolated authority or individual preference. The engineers hold a trusted social role and owe the town honest judgment and protection, but they also owe transparency and patience; overriding residents without explanation fractures the communal bonds that make moral life possible. The residents, in turn, owe openness to those who serve the group, yet their shared fears are a genuine moral fact about the community that cannot be dismissed by credentials alone. Therefore the town must integrate expertise into collective understanding rather than choose between them, honoring both role-based service and the obligation to maintain trust through joint deliberation.

For the judgment, pure expert rule neglects communal inclusion, and pure democratic override neglects the engineers’ serving role, so both carry limited weight, while investing time to build shared understanding best fulfills mutual obligations. For the reasoning, technical knowledge and public trust are both essential social goods embedded in collective life, whereas framing legitimacy as individual consent misses the deeper basis of moral life in shared belonging and reciprocal duty.

